## Approach

I computed backward liveness over the CFG to a fixed point, then for each defining instruction took the live-after set and added edges from the defined variable to every other live variable.

**Successors:** B0→{B1,B3}, B1→{B2,B4}, B2→{B3,B5}, B3→{B4,B1}, B4→{B5,B7}, B5→B6, B6→{B7,B3}, B7→exit.

**Converged live-in sets (after two passes):**
- LiveIn(B7) = {f}
- LiveIn(B6) = {a,c,g,i,j}
- LiveIn(B5) = {a,c,g,i,j}
- LiveIn(B4) = {a,b,c,f,g,i}
- LiveIn(B3) = {a,c,e,f,g}
- LiveIn(B2) = {a,c,d,f,i,j}
- LiveIn(B1) = {a,b,c,d,e,h,j}
- LiveOut(B0) = {a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,j}

**Per-instruction live-after sets** were then computed backward through each block. Each definition contributes edges to every variable live after it. Taken together, these definitions cover every pair of the ten variables a through j. For example, `a = d+1` in B0 links a to b,c,d,e,f,g,h,j, and `i = g+a` in B1 links i to a,b,c,d,f,h,j. Other pairs such as `b`–`h` (from `b = d+a` in B3) and `i`–`j` (from `j = b+g` in B3) are also covered.

Every one of the 45 pairs among {a,…,j} appears, so the interference graph is the complete graph K10.

